# Agent Brief — plain-English companion to engagement.yaml

`engagement.yaml` is what `scope_guard.py` actually enforces (machine-
readable, exact host matching). This file is for humans and for HuntBrain
itself to re-read before Phase 1 — the nuance a flat host list can't
carry: *why* something is out of scope, not just *that* it is, so a scope
decision doesn't get silently re-litigated three phases later.

Copy this to `AGENT-BRIEF.md` (gitignored, same as `engagement.yaml`) at
Phase 0, right after writing `engagement.yaml`. HuntBrain writes this
itself, same as `engagement.yaml` — never ask the user to fill it in by
hand.

---

## Target

`example.com` — [one line: what this company/product is, so a payload
choice makes sense in context, e.g. "B2B SaaS invoicing platform"]

## Why we're testing

[Bug bounty program / signed pentest / personally-owned target — link or
reference. This is the authorization basis, restated in prose so it's
obvious at a glance, not just implied by the presence of engagement.yaml.]

## In scope

See `engagement.yaml`'s `in_scope` list — that's the enforced source of
truth. Restate here only if there's a nuance worth flagging, e.g. "admin
panel is in scope but extremely rate-limited, go slow."

## Out of scope — and why

Don't just copy `engagement.yaml`'s `out_of_scope` list here — explain
*why* each one is excluded, so a future re-read (or a different session
picking this up later) doesn't accidentally treat "not explicitly listed"
as "fair game":

- `internal.example.com` — explicitly excluded by the program, internal
  tooling not covered by the bounty
- `staging-legacy.example.com` — decommissioned, testing it wastes time
  and could alarm whoever still monitors it
- [payment/checkout flow, even though technically in-scope] — client
  asked (outside the written scope doc) not to touch live payment
  processing; note this kind of out-of-band constraint here since
  `engagement.yaml` has no field for it

## Constraints

- Rate limit: [e.g. "1 req/s per host, program-mandated"]
- No DoS, no social engineering, no physical access attempts
- [Any other explicit constraint the program/client gave verbally or in
  writing that isn't captured in engagement.yaml's structured fields]

## Special notes

[Anything else worth a human catching on a quick re-read: known false-
positive traps on this target from a past hunt, a WAF vendor already
identified, test account credentials location, etc.]
